input.body:
The dashboard caches well but still *asks* on every load: an ETag revalidation costs no primary budget, yet it is a request, and a cold entry has nothing to revalidate. A twelve-member sweep is ~75 requests. Unauthenticated that is 60/hour — over budget on the first load, and the rest of the hour renders "unreadable" rows for repos that are fine.

**Ask:** the page should decide up front how much it may spend, and when the budget is low, a full page refresh should make **no requests at all** for a stated window.

Shape:
- a staleness floor (`minAge`) — under pressure a cached entry younger than the floor is served without any request, conditional ones included;
- the floor reaches the rate-limit **reset** when the budget is scarce, so reloading is free until the window rolls;
- a spend ceiling that always reserves the viewer's last requests for the rest of their hour;
- the free `GET /rate_limit` preflight, plus the budget carried across page loads, so a fresh tab plans before it spends rather than discovering the limit by hitting it;
- a 403/429 with nothing left latches, so eleven more members do not each spend a request learning the same thing (requests made are what the secondary limit counts);
- the mode is stated in the UI — a page silently serving hour-old data is worse than one that refuses.

Docs this follows: [rate limits](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/using-the-rest-api/rate-limits-for-the-rest-api), [best practices](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/using-the-rest-api/best-practices-for-using-the-rest-api).

---

**Landed in #1002.** Every point above is implemented in `packs/claudinite-dashboard/budget.mjs`, enforced by `github.mjs`. Measured against a mocked API — anonymous viewer, 45 of 60 left, eight members: cold **42** requests, reload **6** (the members the first load could not afford), reload again **0**.

