6	// credentials this page runs under differ by a factor of eighty-three:
7	//
8	//   unauthenticated   60 requests/hour, per IP address
9	//   a user token   5,000 requests/hour, per user (shared across every app they use)
10	//
11	// A fleet sweep costs roughly six requests per member on a cold cache. Twelve members
12	// is ~72 — over the anonymous budget for the whole hour on the FIRST load, and the
13	// page then spends the rest of that hour rendering "unreadable" rows for repos that
14	// are perfectly fine. Caching alone does not save it, because the existing strategies
15	// all still make a request: an ETag revalidation is free of the PRIMARY limit but it
16	// is still a request, and a cold entry has nothing to revalidate.
17	//
18	// So the policy adds the one thing the cache layer cannot decide on its own: a
19	// STALENESS FLOOR. Under pressure, an entry younger than `minAge` is served without
20	// asking GitHub at all — no request, not even a conditional one. That is what makes
21	// "reload the page and it costs nothing for the next X minutes" true rather than
22	// merely cheap.
23	//
24	// The ladder is driven by HEADROOM — the remaining budget measured in whole page
25	// loads, not in requests — because "1,400 left" means something different to a
26	// one-repo page than to a forty-member fleet.
27	
28	// Requests one member costs on a cold cache: repo metadata, head sha, the
29	// declaration, the tree, one issues page, one runs page. A warm member costs fewer
30	// and a not-adopted one costs three, so this is the ceiling rather than the average —
31	// budgeting against the ceiling is what keeps the last member on the list readable.
32	export const COST_PER_MEMBER = 6;
33	
34	// The page's own fixed overhead: the viewer, plus the canon reference the fleet's
35	// mount column compares against.
36	export const COST_FIXED = 3;
37	
38	export const MINUTE_MS = 60e3;
39	export const HOUR_MS = 3600e3;
40	export const DAY_MS = 24 * HOUR_MS;
41	
42	// What a dashboard load must never do: spend the viewer's LAST requests. Whatever is
43	// held back here is what is left for the rest of their hour — another tab, a CLI, the
44	// next page they open. Ten percent, floored at five so an anonymous 60 keeps six, and
45	// capped at fifty so a 5,000 budget does not hold back five hundred.
46	export const reserveFor = (limit) =>
